gm-method/rendering (a custom mesh that issues its OWN gl draw from onBeforeRender intermittently throws 'glMultiDrawElements/glDrawElements: Insufficient buffer size' and the model collapses to a few stray verts + FPS tanks -- worst on ANGLE/D3D11): ROOT CAUSE is that onBeforeRender fires BEFORE three binds THAT mesh's index/vertex buffers (three binds in renderBufferDirect, after onBeforeRender), so the element buffer bound at your draw time is the PREVIOUS mesh's -- often smaller. A multiDraw range past the bound buffer is a GL_INVALID_OPERATION that the spec makes void the WHOLE call (draws nothing), and it floods every frame until the driver caps error reporting ('Too many GL errors') and the shared context degrades -- everything else on that context (other models, the player) then renders broken too, so the symptom looks like many unrelated bugs from one cause. FIX: the mesh must OWN a GL element buffer (gl.createBuffer + bufferData of its index TypedArray) and bind it itself before the draw, re-uploading only when the index array identity/version changes; do NOT rely on three's binding timing, and do NOT try to guard against the bound buffer's size (querying ELEMENT_ARRAY_BUFFER_BINDING/BUFFER_SIZE in onBeforeRender reads the WRONG, stale buffer -- a guard built on it over-culls valid geometry, the classic mistake). DECISIVE WITNESS: render the suspect object ALONE (hide all other scene children) and gl.getError() after a drained frame -- a non-zero 1282 from the object-in-isolation pins it to that object's draw; then monkeypatch it to own+bind its buffer and confirm the isolated error goes 1282 -> 0 with the draw count unchanged. CRITICAL META-LESSON: a real-GPU/driver bug can be INVISIBLE in a headless ANGLE/SwiftShader context (there it may error-but-still-draw, or not flood), so it reads as unreproducible for many sessions -- the user's chrome://gpu 'Log Messages' section is the witness that breaks the deadlock: it shows the actual driver GL error stream from their machine. Always ask for chrome://gpu (renderer string + Log Messages) when a visual bug is real for the user but every headless measurement says the geometry/state is correct.